aquinas-st-i-ii-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: aquinas-st-i-ii-1.logic.r1 has "◇(in_act ∧ deficient)" and "in_act → can_act" and "deficient → cause_deficient"; aquinas-st-i-ii-1.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-st-i-ii-1.logic.r1.3 have "◇(in_act ∧ deficient)" and "□(in_act → can_act)" and "□(deficient → cause_deficient)"; they differ when only "a thing is in act in a certain respect", "the thing can act", "the thing is in a certain respect deficient in act", "the thing causes a deficient act", "possibly, (a thing is in act in a certain respect and the thing is in a certain respect deficient in act)", "necessarily, (if a thing is in act in a certain respect, then the thing can act)" and "possibly, (a thing is in act in a certain respect and the thing is in a certain respect deficient in act)" are true, which the first allows
aquinas-st-i-ii-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps aquinas-st-i-ii-1.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-st-i-ii-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "a thing is in act in a certain respect", "the thing can act", "the thing is in a certain respect deficient in act", "the thing causes a deficient act", "possibly, (a thing is in act in a certain respect and the thing is in a certain respect deficient in act)", "necessarily, (if a thing is in act in a certain respect, then the thing can act)", "possibly, (a thing is in act in a certain respect and the thing is in a certain respect deficient in act)", "necessarily, (if a thing is in act in a certain respect, then the thing can act)" and "possibly, (a thing is in act in a certain respect and the thing is in a certain respect deficient in act)" are true and "necessarily, (if the thing is in a certain respect deficient in act, then the thing causes a deficient act)" and "necessarily, (if the thing is in a certain respect deficient in act, then the thing causes a deficient act)" are false. Is S2, "2: Nothing hinders a thing from being in act in a certain respect, so that it can act; and in a certain respect deficient in act, so as to cause a deficient act.", true then?
